      <a class="t-card" id="tool-fuel-cost" href="tools/fuel-cost" data-cat="tires-fuel">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-fuel"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">Fuel Cost Calculator</h3><span class="t-verb">Open the calculator<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">Miles, MPG, and the price at the pump give what the trip costs, with the planning cushion already added. Any vehicle works: RV types use their real-world MPG range, and cars, SUVs, pickups and motorcycles use the Energy Department average for that kind of vehicle. Your own measured MPG wins over every preset.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>$552</b> trip fuel, middle</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Best case: $477 at 11 MPG &middot; Worst case: $656 at 8 MPG</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Your own figure: 10.0 MPG</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">An estimate for planning. The pump is the source of truth.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Tires &amp; fuel<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-watts-to-amps" href="tools/watts-to-amps" data-cat="electrical">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-plug"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">Watts to Amps &amp; Shore Power</h3><span class="t-verb">Open the calculator<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">Convert watts and amps either way at 120, 240, or 12 volts, and check the appliances you run at the same time against what your 30-amp or 50-amp hookup actually supplies. The 50-amp cord is 12,000 watts, not 6,000, and the tool shows why.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>12.5 A</b> 1,500 W at 120 volts</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Volts: 120 V &middot; Watts: 1,500 W</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">30-amp service: 3,600 W</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">Typical figures from Cummins' own chart. Your appliance's label wins.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Electrical<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-solar-sizing" href="tools/solar-sizing" data-cat="electrical">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-panel-solar"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">Solar Sizing Calculator</h3><span class="t-verb">Open the calculator<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">Daily watt-hours divided by peak sun hours, with NREL's 0.77 derate applied and the maker-recommended margin shown alongside. The region presets use real peak-sun-hour figures for the country, from about 3 in the Northwest to 6.5 in the desert Southwest.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>623 W</b> panels needed</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Makers' formula: 2,400 Wh / 5.0 hr = 480 W &middot; After the 0.77 derate: 623 W nameplate</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>480 W</b> panels needed</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">A starting point for shopping. Winter sun and shade cut output below the rated hours.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Electrical<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-rv-loan" href="tools/rv-loan" data-cat="planning">
