      <a class="t-card" id="tool-weight-calculator" href="tools/weight-calculator" data-cat="towing">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-scale-balance"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">RV Weight &amp; Towing Calculator</h3><span class="t-verb">Open the calculator<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">Towing capacity vs the 80% rule, door-sticker payload, tongue or pin load, trailer GVWR, per-axle estimates. Type once, everything recomputes, the answer to "can my truck actually tow this?" in three fields.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>5,587 lb</b> loaded trailer</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Towing capacity: 51% of 11,000 lb &middot; Truck payload: 1,070 / 2,200 lb</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Trailer payload: 6,471 / 7,000 lb</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">Estimates only. Always confirm your real load at a certified scale.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Towing &amp; weight<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-rv-toilet-valve" href="tools/rv-toilet-valve" data-cat="water">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-toilet"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">RV Toilet Water Valve Finder</h3><span class="t-verb">Find my valve<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">The toilet water valve is what fails when water is left in the line over a freeze, and it is bought with no part number in hand, because the model label is usually gone. Answer what you can see about your Dometic or Thetford toilet and get the maker's number, with the document it came from.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>385311641</b> water valve kit</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Flush: Foot pedal &middot; Maker: Dometic</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">Not for macerator or cassette toilets.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Water<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-rv-recall-lookup" href="tools/rv-recall-lookup" data-cat="finders">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-checklist"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">RV Recall Lookup</h3><span class="t-verb">Check my unit<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">A recall is filed against a range of VINs, not against a model year, and a motorhome's VIN belongs to the chassis rather than the coach. Put in a VIN and this reads the federal recall and complaint records, or search by year, make and model, and it hands you the maker's lookup where the maker runs one.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>21V685000</b> recall campaign</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Make: Jayco &middot; Model: Jay Flight</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">The United States is live. Canada comes from a snapshot.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Finders<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-rv-anode-rod" href="tools/rv-anode-rod" data-cat="water">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-water-heater"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">RV Water Heater Anode Rod Finder</h3><span class="t-verb">Find my rod<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">Many water heaters have no anode rod, and must not be given one: a Suburban tank is porcelain-lined steel with a sacrificial rod, and an Atwood or Dometic tank is aluminum with a drain plug, which Dometic's own manual says a rod would damage. Answer what you can see and get the part number, or the answer that there is nothing to buy.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>233514</b> anode rod, magnesium</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Brand: Suburban &middot; Tank: Porcelain-lined steel</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">Covers tank heaters, not tankless.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Water<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-rv-converter-finder" href="tools/rv-converter-finder" data-cat="electrical">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-power-cord"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">RV Converter and Power Center Finder</h3><span class="t-verb">Find my replacement<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">The converter in an older RV is usually a model nobody sells any more, and every maker's cross-reference chart is written from its own side. Type the model on the unit and this reads both parsed charts and shows what each maker publishes as the replacement, cited to the chart it came from.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>PD4060KV</b> replaces a Parallax 7355</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Maker: Progressive Dynamics &middot; Fits: 30 amp wall mount</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">WFCO's wider chart is linked rather than read.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Electrical<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-rv-appliance-board-finder" href="tools/rv-appliance-board-finder" data-cat="electrical">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-gears"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">RV Appliance Control Board Finder</h3><span class="t-verb">Find my board<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">A fridge that stops cooling, a generator that will not start, a furnace that will not light: the board is a common cause, though check the simple things first. Dinosaur Electronics publishes the only public charts pairing a maker's model or part number with the board that replaces it, across four charts. Where a model was built with more than one board, every production range is shown rather than one being picked for you.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>Micro P-711</b> replaces a Dometic board</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Fits: RM 2652, by range &middot; Norcold: control + eyebrow</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">Fridges, generators and ignitors. All four charts read.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Electrical<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-rv-water-filter" href="tools/rv-water-filter" data-cat="water">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-faucet"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">RV Water Filter Finder</h3><span class="t-verb">Find my filter<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">A water filter either screws onto the hose or sits in a housing, and which one you can use turns on the thread at the inlet and the size of the cartridge coming out of the housing. Answer what you can see and this lands on the maker's figures for an inline filter or a cartridge format, or says that the maker does not publish the figure you asked for.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>Slim Line</b> 10 inch by 2.5 inch</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">End cap: Double open end &middot; Big Blue: 4.5 inch, will not fit</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">2.5 inch is not 4.5 inch, and 10 is not 20.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Water<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-battery-runtime" href="tools/battery-runtime" data-cat="electrical">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-battery"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">Battery Runtime Calculator</h3><span class="t-verb">Open the calculator<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">How long the house batteries carry your gear before they need charging. The amp-hours on the label, the chemistry, and the watts you draw: lead-acid gives half its amp-hours, lithium most of them, and the tool applies the right fraction.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>17.1 hr</b> runtime estimate</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Label says: 100 Ah = 1,280 Wh &middot; Usable at 80%: 1,024 Wh</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Usable at 50%: 1,200 Wh</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">Label figures and maker-published fractions. A battery monitor is the source of truth.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Electrical<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-tire-date-code" href="tools/tire-date-code" data-cat="tires-fuel">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-tire"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">Tire Date Code Decoder</h3><span class="t-verb">Open the decoder<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">A tire made since 2000 carries the week and year it was made in the last four digits of its DOT code. Type those four digits and get the date and your tire's age, with the six-to-10-year replacement window NHTSA cites.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>1024</b> date digits</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Built in the: tenth week &middot; Year: 2024</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>2321</b> date digits</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">Reads the date only. It cannot judge whether a tire is safe.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Tires &amp; fuel<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-snow-load" href="tools/snow-load" data-cat="towing">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-snow-shovel"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">Roof Snow Load Calculator</h3><span class="t-verb">Open the calculator<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">Snow's weight is just the weight of the water in it. Enter how deep it is on the roof, or its water equivalent if you have one, and this gives you the load in pounds per square foot, and compares it to your roof's rating.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>7.5 lb/ft&#178;</b> load carried</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">Against your rating: 25% of 30 lb/ft&#178; &middot; Snow on the roof: 6.0 inches</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>15.0 lb/ft&#178;</b> load carried</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">Not a structural assessment.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Towing &amp; weight<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>
      <a class="t-card" id="tool-state-trailer-brake-laws" href="tools/state-trailer-brake-laws" data-cat="towing">
        <div class="t-top">
          <span class="t-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="/assets/img/icons/sprite.svg#ic-stop"/></svg></span>
          <div class="t-hd"><h3 class="t-name">State Trailer Brake Laws</h3><span class="t-verb">Look up my state<span class="t-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-copy">Whether a trailer has to have brakes is set by the state you tow in, not by a national rule. The 3,000 pound figure everyone repeats is a federal exception written for commercial vehicles. Pick a state and this shows the threshold its own vehicle code states, quoted, with the section named and linked to the state's site.</div>
        <div class="t-spec">
        <p class="t-spec-l"><b>3,000 lb</b> the number everyone repeats</p>
        <p class="t-spec-l">California: 1,500 lb &middot; Idaho: 1,500 lb unladen</p>
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">Every state read so far, each from its own code.</p>
        <div class="t-go" aria-hidden="true">Towing &amp; weight<span class="t-chev">&rsaquo;</span></div>
      </a>